          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What sort of dietary change does guidance describe for high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance gives only a general outline of dietary advice for high blood pressure. I could not get the specific details you asked about, because my search tools ran out of capacity before they returned them.\n\n**What the guidance says:** The \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\" summary (a major cardiology society consensus document) says every major body recommends the same lifestyle measures, whether or not medicine is also used. The dietary ones are:\n\n- Reducing dietary sodium (salt).\n- Eating a pattern rich in vegetables, fruit, whole grains and low-fat dairy, and low in saturated fat. This is the kind of eating plan the DASH approach describes, though that name did not appear in the passage I retrieved.\n\nThe same document says each of these measures gives a meaningful reduction in blood pressure on its own, and that their effects add together. The passage does not give figures for how much blood pressure falls, so I can't say how large the effect is.\n\n**What I could not retrieve:**\n- A specific sodium target.\n- Details of the DASH eating pattern.\n- Potassium intake guidance, including any cautions for people with kidney disease or taking certain medicines.\n- Any differences between authorities on diet. The document I found does have a section on disagreements, but I could not retrieve it, so I cannot report what it says.\n\nIf you want these details, a clinician or a dietitian can explain how the recommendations apply to you. If you have kidney problems or take medicines that affect potassium, ask about potassium before changing your diet.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
